course: dictionary
lesson: 13368
title: The word hassle
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Hassle is an informal word, so you hear it in everyday talk more than in school books. As a noun it means something annoying and difficult that takes effort, or trouble and arguing. «Getting the bus in the rain is such a hassle.» The clues are the rain and the effort it takes.
explain: As a verb, to hassle someone means to keep annoying them or asking them to do something. «Stop hassling me about my homework!» The word hassling shows that Sam wants the nagging to stop. Always ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Some phrases use hassle in a helpful way. «No hassle» means without trouble, and «hassle-free» means with no problems at all. «The train trip was hassle-free, so we arrived on time.»
explain: If someone keeps hassling you online or in the street, that is never your fault. Tell a trusted adult straight away.
fact: Hassle is an informal word, so it is used in chatty, everyday speech.
fact: As a noun, «hassle» means something annoying and difficult that takes effort.
fact: As a noun, «hassle» can also mean trouble or arguing, as in «I don't want any hassle».
fact: As a verb, «hassle» means to keep annoying someone or asking them to do something.
fact: If someone keeps hassling you online or in the street, tell a trusted adult.
exercise: «Getting the bus to school in the rain is such a hassle.» What does hassle mean here? | A fun trip to a new place | A quick, free ride | A kind bus driver | *Something annoying and difficult that takes effort
exercise: «I don't want any hassle, so I will just say sorry.» What does hassle mean here? | *Trouble or arguing | A sweet snack | A long holiday | A party with loud music
exercise: Sam says, «Stop hassling me about my homework!» What does hassling mean? | Running very fast | *Keep annoying someone or asking them to do something | Giving someone a present | Helping someone with a hard sum
test: Mum says the forms for the school trip are a real hassle. What does that mean? | *Something annoying and difficult that takes effort | Something very exciting | A small, quiet room | A kind surprise
test: If someone keeps hassling you online, what should you do? | Ignore your family and friends | *Tell a trusted adult | Reply with a nasty message | Keep it secret and hope it stops
exam: Which sentence uses hassle as a verb? | I don't want any hassle | Our trip was hassle-free | *Stop hassling me about my homework | Getting the bus is such a hassle
